1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,327
On the tenth of October, Punjab's government said around eight thousand brick kilns had been converted to zigzag technology.

2
00:00:13,249 --> 00:00:15,912
The province counts about ten thousand kilns.

3
00:00:15,912 --> 00:00:19,403
Nobody outside the government can check which ones changed.

4
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:24,158
This is Bhatta.

5
00:00:24,158 --> 00:00:30,198
It checks that claim from space, one kiln at a time, and keeps the answer on a public ledger.

6
00:00:33,213 --> 00:00:37,372
An old kiln is an oval trench with a chimney in the middle.

7
00:00:37,372 --> 00:00:43,013
A zigzag kiln is a rectangle that forces the air through the bricks in a zigzag.

8
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:53,279
Field measurements in Pakistan found the zigzag design cuts black carbon by about sixty percent.

9
00:00:57,154 --> 00:01:00,309
But this is what a satellite sees at ten metres.

10
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:04,057
The oval and the rectangle blur into the same few pixels.

11
00:01:05,909 --> 00:01:09,263
So Bhatta never certifies a conversion from space.

12
00:01:09,263 --> 00:01:12,013
When the image cannot decide, it says so.

13
00:01:17,015 --> 00:01:21,557
We scanned twenty two districts of Punjab with October's Sentinel 2 images.

14
00:01:21,557 --> 00:01:24,705
Eight thousand eight hundred and seventy nine kilns.

15
00:01:26,961 --> 00:01:30,987
One thousand three hundred and sixteen still show the old oval shape.

16
00:01:30,987 --> 00:01:35,364
Five thousand two hundred and fifty five cannot be decided from ten metres.

17
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:46,485
Each column is a kiln. Red means the image still shows the old technology.

18
00:01:49,424 --> 00:01:54,716
Open one. At ninety percent confidence, this image supports only one answer:

19
00:01:54,716 --> 00:01:55,621
the old kiln.

20
00:01:57,448 --> 00:02:03,247
On kilns the model never saw, about one call in twenty seven like this one was wrong.

21
00:02:03,247 --> 00:02:04,407
The page says so.

22
00:02:07,501 --> 00:02:12,920
Here the image cannot separate the two designs. Bhatta does not guess.

23
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,623
It sends an inspector.

24
00:02:18,524 --> 00:02:23,679
Every result for the season is committed to Ethereum Sepolia as one Merkle root.

25
00:02:23,679 --> 00:02:28,576
Your browser rebuilds this kiln's record and checks it against the contract.

26
00:02:28,576 --> 00:02:30,896
You do not have to trust our server.

27
00:02:36,745 --> 00:02:40,190
Inspectors sign their findings with their own keys.

28
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:46,741
In this demonstration, a test inspector signs zigzag confirmed on a kiln the satellite calls old.

29
00:02:47,329 --> 00:02:51,241
The contract records the conflict in public, and nobody can delete it.

30
00:02:56,565 --> 00:03:01,127
A certificate needs an inspector's signature and no satellite contradiction.

31
00:03:01,127 --> 00:03:06,349
When the satellite record contradicts it, the certificate is withdrawn, also in public.

32
00:03:11,679 --> 00:03:15,130
For a district office, Bhatta ranks where to go first:

33
00:03:15,130 --> 00:03:20,242
conflicts, then kilns the satellite contradicts, then the ones it cannot decide.

34
00:03:26,011 --> 00:03:30,022
The tests were written before the data. Finding kilns passed.

35
00:03:30,022 --> 00:03:33,376
Telling the two designs apart at ten metres missed.

36
00:03:33,376 --> 00:03:37,386
We show the misses, because they are why the product refuses.

37
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:47,267
Anyone can rebuild the season's root from the published data and compare it with the chain.

38
00:03:53,610 --> 00:03:56,166
It works in English and Urdu, on a phone.

39
00:04:00,598 --> 00:04:01,080
Bhatta.

40
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:08,028
Public satellite data, a public ledger, and an honest answer for Punjab's inspectors and kiln owners.
